### How does InheritableThreadLocal work?

```java
InheritableThreadLocal<String> ctx = new InheritableThreadLocal<>();
ctx.set("REQ-123");
new Thread(() -> System.out.println(ctx.get())).start();  // prints REQ-123
```

### How do you override childValue?

```java
InheritableThreadLocal<List<String>> tags = new InheritableThreadLocal<>() {
    @Override
    protected List<String> childValue(List<String> parentValue) {
        return new ArrayList<>(parentValue);  // defensive copy
    }
};
```

### Why is it dangerous in thread pools?

Inheritance happens in the Thread constructor, not at submission. Pool workers are created once and reused.

```java
InheritableThreadLocal<String> user = new InheritableThreadLocal<>();
user.set("admin");  // whoever built the pool
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);  // workers inherit "admin"

user.set("rajesh");
pool.submit(() -> System.out.println(user.get()));  // prints "admin" (stale!)
```

### What is the correct pattern for pools?

Capture on the submitting thread, set on the worker, and always remove in a finally block.

```java
String current = user.get();  // capture on submitting thread
pool.submit(() -> {
    user.set(current);         // set explicitly on the worker
    try {
        doWork();
    } finally {
        user.remove();         // ALWAYS clear to prevent leakage
    }
});
```

### What are the two failure modes?

| Failure | Cause |
|---------|-------|
| Staleness | Task sees the pool creator's value, not the submitter's |
| Leakage | A value left by one task bleeds into the next task on the same worker |

